at the education secretary gavin  williams and joining us once again from  westminster  very good morning minister good morning  to you  it is i'm afraid to say another day and  had another u-turn from the department  for education what has led to this one  well what we've always said and i'm sure  you'll go back to when we first issued  the guidance back in may  as schools were returning uh welcoming  back those first set of pupils for the  first of june  we said this would be something that  would keep under constant review  then when we issued for further guidance  for full return of schools  back in early july again we emphasized  the importance of keeping this area  under review  and actually having had the opportunity  as we saw the  world health organization put out the  details of  their their thoughts on this one of the  key areas that they recommended in areas  of  high transmission that there is maybe  some worth of wearing face masks  in those areas of high transmission in  education settings  now we're very fortunate as you're aware  and you've reported on repeatedly  actually right across the country we're  seeing that  the level of covert transmission fall  dramatically  so many parts of the country are  increasingly safe but there  are some areas of a country where we've  had to do local lockdowns  and we think that it is right in those  few areas where we've had to do local  lockdowns  that it is right that secondary schools  in communal areas that youngsters  do wear face coverings only in those  secondary schools in those local  lockdown areas  and of course we were listening to the  who advice on that  yeah the the w the world health  organization advice changed on saturday  we spoke to jenny harris the deputy  chief medical officer  on the program on monday and asked her  directly about it why haven't we changed  she said the following if you look at  the world health organization it's a  consensus statement  still because the evidence is not strong  it also identifies poor compliance  doesn't recommend covering should be  worn when playing she carried on  that was what the deputy chief medical  officer was saying on monday so again  i'll ask  what has changed what is the evidence  that you are relying on we always hear  from you that you follow the signs  specifically what is the evidence that  this is something that needed to be  brought in because you know you've had  four days  to come up with this uh but we always  follow and listen to the best scientific  medical advice and that's why we're not  recommending that face covering should  be mandatory  uh right across the country in all  schools because uh  the best scientific and medical advice  says that that isn't necessary  that was the deputy chief medical  officer who has access to the same level  of information and possibly one would  assume  uh given our academic background with a  greater deal of comprehension than you  and she was saying  on monday that this was not something  that we needed to bring in  so i ask again what has changed because  to be honest with you once again  it looks like you've been bumped into  this by the scottish government as with  exam results  well as as as you highlighted what uh  jenny did sort of highlight the fact  that we shouldn't be bringing  uh mandatory face coverings in uh  communal areas  right across the country into every  secondary school  and that's not what we're doing but we  are recognizing the fact that there are  certain areas of a country where there's  a  high instance of uh or higher instance  of uh  coronavirus that we're taking in extra  precautionary measure  and when we do look at school return the  key thing the thing that has informed us  most of  all yes this is for scientific and  medical advice  and it's been on the basis of how we  ensure  that all schools are as safe as possible  there as good environment as possible in  order to welcome children back  and as we look towards next week and the  following week as more and more schools  come back  we want to create an environment that  has  safety at the absolute core of  everything that we do  because the safety pupils for safety for  those who work in schools  is absolutely critical for all of us uh  but i'm sorry the overwhelming number of  the lack  of factor in the department for  education making making decisions such  as these  it does seem to be something of a  problem i'll repeat on saturday  the who changed its advice on monday the  deputy chief medical officer said we  didn't have to change  anything as regards face mass you  repeated those comments monday afternoon  tuesday morning yesterday morning we had  the business secretary saying  on the program you know there's  absolutely no need for us to do this you  know the science is not there  you're not telling me it was a  coincidence that the scottish government  the northern irish government changed  their advice and then  then the government in westminster then  happens to do the same  well as as you um uh will have sort of  noted what we're recommending in terms  of mandating for wearing  face coverings in communal areas for  secondary schools  is only in a very very small number of  areas  in the country which are in local  lockdown and what we're doing is we  recognize that there are added concerns  in those communities about transmission  but we also recognize how important it  is for every child  to get back and as i'm sure you are sort  of  conscious of as well is it's important  that we get  all children back and all schools open  not just in those areas that  aren't in lockdown but also those  schools that are in areas where there is  lockdown  and making sure that we look at the  advice closely  act on uh that advice but making sure  that we do it in a sensible and  pragmatic way  that's why we're not mandating the  wearing of face coverings in  uh every school in the country we're not  mandating instead what we're doing is  we're passing responsibility for that  decision to head teachers who already  have so much on their plate in terms of  getting kids back into school catching  up on learning now we're asking them  to act as public health officials and  decide whether or not  face mass should be mandatory in their  school on what basis  are we asking them to do that well what  we've been consistent on in terms of our  advice is that  actually there isn't a need to be  wearing face masks in uh  in school can just change change their  mind  you're giving the responsibility to the  head teachers well we've always had a  system in this country where  there's a loss of uh devolved  responsibility for head teachers  and of course as you'll go around in the  middle of the mistake as you'll go  around  the whole school state in this country  uh every school  tends to be of a different shape and a  different size and we do recognize that  sort of head teachers will sometimes  need to show  an element of discretion that's why a  few weeks ago as i'm sure you also  picked up  is the fact that actually we made the  decision to make sure that  face coverings were mandatory on home to  school transport  because we felt that was the best route  in order to be able to sort of not only  to  minimize the spread of transmission  among pupils and  in the wider community but also making  sure that there's real confidence about  uh for children to be able to return to  school  these are all measures that at every  step  is about making sure there's the safety  of those young people  safety of those people who teach in  schools  and ensuring that actually all children  are welcomed back into school  in september that's our priority that's  our focus as a government it's a  national priority  indeed okay do you think that parents  pupils  teachers head teachers have faith in  your ability to see us through this mr  williamson  statistics out today showing that the  report out today upon the attainment gap  in our schools the latest data contained  in this report for  2019 since then the covet epidemic has  had a profound  impact on english education everything  we have learned so far  during the lockdown suggests the  response to the health christ crisis  will have a  particularly adverse impact on poor and  vulnerable children  disadvantaged kids at the end by the  time they take their gcses were before  the pandemic  18 months behind those from rather more  affluent  backgrounds isn't the simple truth of it  all mr williamson that the government  was  failing in terms of education before the  viral pandemic  well what you'd seen uh over the last 10  years you've been seeing a closing of  the attainment gap  that's why that's why today we're able  to sort of say that you know  from september you're going to be seeing  another 50 new free schools  that have been really driving academic  excellence often in some of the most  deprived areas of a country  and actually the focus in terms of  driving standards whether  it's the phonics revolution in terms of  giving  youngsters the best start in life all of  these actions are having a real  impact in terms of children's attainment  but you're right to say  is there more to do there is so much  more to do that's why the focus is  constantly there to  drive stanzas and push more uh push  further and what you've seen over the  last few months  uh not only have you seen a sort of a  you know  effectively a setting up of almost a  parallel education system with uh  you know the open national academy  bringing some of the  very best sorry i didn't mean to speak  across you sorry  but i didn't mean to be rude there no no  no i'm sorry and i'm sorry  similarly but the data says the gap  between rich and poor kids  has stopped closing the experts behind  this report say it will increase if we  want a demonstration  of the situation we're in at the moment  yesterday we saw the head of off paul  resigning a couple of days ago we saw  you being nominated for  mp of the year so so  every every bit of the government's  focus  it recognizes that we've got to get  children back into school  the best way for children to be able to  from the most disadvantaged backgrounds  to close ascertainment gap is to see  them back  into school that's why we're putting  that focus of  getting children back into school  in september what every single year  group we saw over 1.6 million children  back in school  before the summer holidays we want to  welcome all children  and that is the best way of closing the  attainment gap making sure that we have  children  from every background including and most  importantly from the most disadvantaged  background  back in the classroom sat in front of  their teachers who will inspire them and  give them the encouragement and the  support in order to achieve the very  best in themselves  that's why we're going to be getting  every child back into the classroom  in september that's our focus that's  what we're going to deliver on  gavin williamson good to see you thank  you thank you for having me cheers

